CONFIDENTIAL - do not forward

Dear Chief Executive,

You asked for a short summary of investigation HR-INV-2026-014 now that it has closed. The full report (48 pages) is held in the people team's case file.

1. What was alleged

On 17 March 2026 the Deputy Branch Manager at Nettlebridge, Osgood Pereira-Lang, reported that overtime records for the branch did not match the door access log. The concern was that a Team Leader, Corwin Tsakalidis (grade Q4), had altered the overtime sheets of three counter staff after they had been signed, so that hours were added which had not been worked.

2. How it was investigated

Because the Branch Manager at Nettlebridge had countersigned the sheets, I appointed an external investigator, Odalys Brennagh of Brennagh Workplace Inquiries, on 23 March 2026. She interviewed 6 witnesses and Mr Tsakalidis, compared 11 weeks of overtime sheets (5 January to 22 March 2026) with door access and Halyard log-in records, and reported on 28 April 2026. Her fee was $6,840.

3. What was found

- 23 entries on the overtime sheets had been changed after signature.
- The changes added 61.5 hours in total and led to overpayments of $2,318.40 across the three staff.
- Mr Tsakalidis accepted that he made the changes. He said he was "making good" unpaid work the three staff had done opening the branch early during the heating failure in January. The investigator found that some early opening did happen but that it amounted to no more than 19 hours.
- There was no evidence that Mr Tsakalidis gained personally, and no evidence that the three staff knew of the changes.
- The Branch Manager countersigned without checking. This was found to be a failure of supervision, not misconduct.

4. Outcome

A disciplinary hearing was held on 5 May 2026, chaired by the Branch Manager of Oudeveld. The outcome, confirmed in writing on 6 May 2026, is:

- a final written warning for Mr Tsakalidis, to remain on file for 14 months;
- removal of his authority to sign overtime sheets for 6 months;
- no recovery from the three staff. The Society will pay them for the 19 hours actually worked and write off the rest. The overpayment relating to hours not worked was 61.5 less 19, that is 42.5 hours;
- refresher training for the Branch Manager at Nettlebridge on payroll controls.

5. Appeal

Mr Tsakalidis has 8 working days from 6 May 2026 to appeal. Under the disciplinary procedure, an appeal is heard by the Area Director for the area in which the employee's branch is located. If the Area Director is unavailable, I will ask another Area Director to hear it.

6. Control changes

From 1 June 2026 overtime in all branches will be entered directly in the payroll system by the member of staff and approved electronically; paper sheets will be withdrawn. I have asked the Head of Internal Audit to include overtime in the 2027 audit plan.

7. Who knows

This summary has gone only to you and the Society Secretary. Please do not share it further. The names of the three counter staff are in the full report and are deliberately left out here.

